Private key

64 bytes, two independent 32-byte keys concatenated.

    offset  size  field
    0       32    X25519 private key
    32      32    Ed25519 private key

The X25519 half is a raw scalar. Identity stores the bytes exactly as
supplied and returns them unchanged; clamping happens inside the
curve implementation when the scalar is used.

    load_private_key       stores prv_bytes verbatim   RNS/Identity.py:744
    get_private_key        returns them verbatim       RNS/Identity.py:722
    curve25519 clamps      at exchange and base mult   RNS/Cryptography/X25519.py:78

Two consequences, both recorded as vectors in test/identity/005 and
test/identity/006:

    An unclamped scalar round-trips through Identity unchanged. An
    implementation that clamps on load will export a different private
    key than it imported.

    The public key, and therefore the identity hash, is derived after
    clamping. An unclamped scalar and its clamped form yield the same
    public key and the same identity hash.

The Ed25519 half is a seed in the sense of RFC 8032, not an expanded
secret scalar.

The two halves are unrelated. Neither is derived from the other, and
there is no master seed.

    RNS/Identity.py:744, RNS/Cryptography/Ed25519.py:34,
    RNS/Cryptography/X25519.py:78


Public key

64 bytes, in the same order.

    offset  size  field
    0       32    X25519 public key
    32      32    Ed25519 public key

    RNS/Identity.py:773


Identity hash

    identity_hash = SHA-256(public_key)[0:16]

16 bytes. Derived from the public key only. The private key does not
enter the derivation.

    RNS/Identity.py:783


Signature

Ed25519 over the message bytes, 64 bytes, as specified in RFC 8032.
Signing is deterministic: the same key and message always produce the
same signature.

    RNS/Identity.py:907


Vector format

kind: identity, determinism: encode

raw is one line:

    line 1    public key, 64 bytes

expect names the public key, its two halves, and the identity hash.

raw carries the public key rather than the private key because the
Reticulum-specific facts are the concatenation order and the hash.
Deriving a public key from a private one is X25519 and Ed25519, which
are standard primitives with their own published vectors; this corpus
does not restate them.

The private-to-public derivation is covered by a separate kind,
keyset, which is generated once dump can verify it. The clamping pair
described above belongs there: as identity vectors the two cases would
be byte-identical, which is precisely the property they exist to
record.


Note on the upstream signature vector

The upstream test at tests/identity.py:35 signs

    signed_message.encode("utf-8")

where signed_message is a 1224-character hex string. The signed
message is therefore the ASCII text of that hex, not the 612 bytes it
represents. Vectors carrying that value record the message as the hex
of its utf-8 bytes, so the distinction cannot be lost.
